Research Questions & Hypotheses

Research Question 1

Do rural fatal crashes involve more fatalities per crash than urban fatal crashes, and how are crash, road, and driver characteristics related to this difference?

Null hypothesis: There is no difference in the number of fatalities per crash between rural and urban crashes.

Alternative hypothesis: Rural fatal crashes involve a greater number of fatalities per crash than urban fatal crashes.

Independent variable: Geographic location (RUR_URB)

Dependent variable: Number of fatalities (FATALS)

Key predictors: RUR_URB, VSPD_LIM, weather, LGT_COND, HOUR, driver age, DRINKING, TRAV_SP, rollover, FUNC_SYS, SPEEDREL

Research Question 2

Within the set of fatal crashes, are crashes more common on weekends than weekdays, and do the timing and types of vehicles involved differ between weekend and weekday crashes?

Null hypothesis: There is no difference in the frequency of fatal crashes between weekends and weekdays, nor in timing/vehicle type.

Alternative hypothesis: The frequency of fatal crashes differs between weekends and weekdays, and timing/vehicle type also differ.

Dependent variable: Weekend indicator (weekend vs. weekday)

Key predictors: HOUR, LGT_COND, RUR_URB, STATE, VPICBODY, speed-related indicators

Research Question 3

For fatal crashes, which state, crash, vehicle, and driver characteristics are most strongly associated with whether a crash occurs on a weekend and at night versus a weekday and/or daytime?

Null hypothesis: There is no association between state, crash, vehicle, and driver characteristics and whether a fatal crash occurs on a weekend or at night.

Alternative hypothesis: State, crash, vehicle, and driver characteristics are significantly associated with whether a fatal crash occurs on a weekend or at night.

Dependent variable A: Weekend (weekend vs. weekday)

Dependent variable B: Night vs. day (using HOUR/LGT_COND)

Key predictors: STATE, RUR_URB, weather, LGT_COND, HOUR, TRAV_SP, SPEEDREL, rollover, DRINKING, age, sex

Literature Review

(Carried over from progress report — Precious & David: add a connecting sentence at the end tying these back to the final results/discussion.)

(Shrestha and Shrestha, 2017) Factors associated with crash severities in built-up areas along rural highways of Nevada. Examined over 300 crashes using binary logistic regression; found speeding and nighttime driving were significant factors.

(Liu and Subramanian, 2009) Factors Related to Fatal Single-Vehicle Run-Off-Road Crashes. Used FARS data; found rural roads, speeding, curves, alcohol use, fatigue, and adverse weather were significant factors.

(Zwerling et al., 2005) Fatal motor vehicle crashes in rural and urban areas: decomposing rates into contributing factors. Found the higher rural fatality rate is associated with greater crash severity rather than higher crash frequency.

(Ferenchak and Ghodrat Abadi, 2021) Nighttime pedestrian fatalities. Found nighttime pedestrian fatalities increased most on urban arterial roads, non-intersection locations, and in crashes involving SUVs.

(Saha et al., 2016) Adverse weather conditions and fatal motor vehicle crashes in the United States, 1994-2012. Found weather-related fatalities decreased over the period studied, with state-level variation.

